Ladybird accepts SVG canvas image sources with missing natural sizes,
resolves their concrete size against the output bitmap, and rasterizes
at that size before drawing. Explicit zero natural dimensions remain
unusable. Element width and height attributes don’t supply the image
size for drawImage(). This follows the HTML image-usability rules and
the CSS default-sizing algorithm used by the drawing-images steps.

Blink resolves SVG sizes through ImageElementBase::ElementSize and
GetSourceImageForCanvas, using SVGImageForContainer::ConcreteObjectSize
with the default object size. WebKit resolves them in imageElementSize
and CanvasDrawImageSizing::resolve, passing the output bitmap size from
CanvasRenderingContext2DBase. Both accept dimensionless SVG sources and
use the output bitmap for the missing-size fallback, as Ladybird does.

Gecko accepts vector sources in nsLayoutUtils::SurfaceFromElement and
passes deferred vector drawing data to CanvasRenderingContext2D::
DrawImage. Its missing intrinsic dimensions fall back to 300 by 150 in
that path. Ladybird instead follows the output-bitmap fallback required
by HTML, matching Blink and WebKit for differently sized canvases.

The saved optimitech.com slider uses drawImage() with SVG backgrounds
that have a viewBox but no width or height. Chrome draws those images.
The canvas regression also checks explicit dimensions, one missing
natural dimension, no ratio, zero sizes, source crops, transformed
output, element attributes, and SVG image elements.
